From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rdoupe
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 03:25:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/24] Let another power manager own the profile on plug and
 unplug

The battery service re-applies the remembered AC or battery profile on every
power-source change. On machines where a vendor daemon or the user's own tool
manages the profile, the two fight over each transition and the wrong profile
is briefly applied. A powerprofiles-autoswitch-off toggle now makes those
source-driven restores (and the login autodetect) a no-op, while a profile
chosen explicitly still applies and is remembered.

Backport to v4.5.0-rc1: the toggle check goes ahead of the ASUS ROG USB-C source
resolution added by the previous backport, so a skipped restore never probes
charge_mode.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5
Backport-of: 308e4a372f72cc09191b73c22196b4680a5f8bda
Backport-of: 524481be00a132f117b6b04c40bcb8816ff22c75 (fixup)

(cherry picked from commit 308e4a372f72cc09191b73c22196b4680a5f8bda)
---
 bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-set          |  8 ++++++++
 manual/36-system-sleep.md              |  2 ++
 test/shell.d/powerprofiles-set-test.sh | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 28 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-set b/bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-set
index 6358bc53..a6afc2ad 100755
--- a/bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-set
+++ b/bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-set
@@ -29,6 +29,14 @@ case "$action" in
   *) usage ;;
 esac
 
+# Restoring the remembered profile on a power-source change (no profile named)
+# is skipped when another tool manages the profile, such as a vendor power
+# daemon, so the two do not fight over every plug and unplug. A profile chosen
+# explicitly is still applied and remembered.
+if [[ -z $requested_profile ]] && omarchy-toggle-enabled powerprofiles-autoswitch-off; then
+  exit 0
+fi
+
 # An ASUS ROG laptop runs at full power only from its own barrel adapter. A
 # USB-C charger delivers far less (100 W, against 200-330 W from the
 # barrel), so there it saves power as on battery: balanced unless a profile was
diff --git a/manual/36-system-sleep.md b/manual/36-system-sleep.md
index f981fd01..611fb3a0 100644
--- a/manual/36-system-sleep.md
+++ b/manual/36-system-sleep.md
@@ -10,6 +10,8 @@ On a laptop, Omarchy remembers your power profile separately for plugged in and
 
 You can see what your machine offers with `omarchy powerprofiles list`, and set the one you want for the state you're currently in with `omarchy powerprofiles set autodetect power-saver`. To set the other state without unplugging anything, name it directly: `omarchy powerprofiles set battery power-saver`. Whatever you pick is what you'll get back the next time you're in that state. On an ASUS ROG laptop, a USB-C charger is a state of its own, `usb-c`, apart from the barrel adapter's `ac`: it defaults to balanced, and `omarchy powerprofiles set usb-c performance` sets it from anywhere.
 
+If another tool manages power profiles for you, such as a vendor power daemon, stop Omarchy from switching on plug and unplug with `mkdir -p ~/.local/state/omarchy/toggles && touch ~/.local/state/omarchy/toggles/powerprofiles-autoswitch-off`. Profiles you pick yourself still apply; remove the file to switch automatically again.
+
 ### Toggle suspend
 
 You toggle suspend by running `omarchy toggle suspend` from the terminal. That just reveals/hides the option under _System_ (or `Super + Esc`), and then you can see if it works consistently on your system. If not, you can hide it again with the same command.
diff --git a/test/shell.d/powerprofiles-set-test.sh b/test/shell.d/powerprofiles-set-test.sh
index b3c1a2ca..5cbf596e 100755
--- a/test/shell.d/powerprofiles-set-test.sh
+++ b/test/shell.d/powerprofiles-set-test.sh
@@ -38,6 +38,9 @@ export OMARCHY_POWERPROFILES_STATE_DIR="$tmp_dir/state"
 # No ASUS charge_mode here, so an ASUS ROG laptop running this on USB-C power gets the
 # same results as any other machine.
 export OMARCHY_SYS_ROOT="$tmp_dir/sys"
+# Toggle flags live under $HOME; keep the developer's own out of the test.
+export HOME="$tmp_dir/home"
+mkdir -p "$HOME"
 
 "$ROOT/bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-set" ac balanced
 [[ $(<"$tmp_dir/state/ac") == "balanced" ]] || fail "power profile stores AC preference"
@@ -76,6 +79,21 @@ pass "power profile retains performance as AC default"
 [[ $(tail -n 1 "$tmp_dir/calls") == "power-saver" ]] || fail "init restores the autodetected preference"
 pass "power profile init restores the autodetected preference"
 
+mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/state/omarchy/toggles"
+touch "$HOME/.local/state/omarchy/toggles/powerprofiles-autoswitch-off"
+calls_before=$(wc -l <"$tmp_dir/calls")
+"$ROOT/bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-set" ac
+ON_BATTERY=1 "$ROOT/bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-set"
+"$ROOT/bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-init"
+(( $(wc -l <"$tmp_dir/calls") == calls_before )) ||
+  fail "power source changes leave the profile alone when autoswitch is off" "$(tail -n 3 "$tmp_dir/calls")"
+pass "power source changes leave the profile alone when autoswitch is off"
+
+"$ROOT/bin/omarchy-powerprofiles-set" battery balanced
+[[ $(tail -n 1 "$tmp_dir/calls") == "balanced" && $(<"$tmp_dir/state/battery") == "balanced" ]] ||
+  fail "an explicit profile still applies when autoswitch is off"
+pass "an explicit profile still applies when autoswitch is off"
+
 rg -F '["omarchy-powerprofiles-set", pendingPowerSource]' "$ROOT/shell/plugins/services/battery/Service.qml" >/dev/null ||
   fail "battery service applies profiles through Omarchy command"
 pass "battery service applies profiles through Omarchy command"
